th mr gao um i'll  let you have the floor  if we deal with history as victor has  raised then we will have to deal with  history as facts rather than fiction  if we compare the last four british  governors with the four chief executives  since hong kong become chinese again  we can see that all four  of the last british governors  receive  very very high popular rating  there was no democratic  formal democratic accountability of the  british governors to hong kong but every  one of them was so responsive to public  opinions that every one of them receive  very high rating  none of that applies to the four chief  executives of hong kong  there was no british governor who had to  go to hong kong with 7 000 security  service people present to make sure that  there were no incidents that just  happened with the election of john lee  well coming back to present day i i want  to get to johnny in just a moment but i  also want to take a look at carrie  lamb's legacy you've spoken there steve  about approval ratings now i see carrie  lam is leaving officers the least  popular chief executive ever  33.4 percent approval i mean arguably it  was the 2019 protests that caused xi  jinping to harden his stance on hong  kong and much of that people believe was  due to lam's handling of the extradition  bill much of that driven some might  argue by her own political ambitions  tom you're in hong kong how will people  remember her  well um somewhat of a disastrous legacy  in that we have seen three news outlets  uh closed in the last year or two bear  in mind  um john lee was the number two official  right much of the last year and the  security chief before that overseeing  the protest  um 60 plus civil society groups  disbanded last year  most of the opposition the democrats are  either behind bars have fled into  ex-help exile or have quit politics  altogether we're seeing a mass exodus in  light of the national security law and  harsh coveted rules that lam has also  been accused of mishandling protests are  effectively illegal in the city at the  moment and  about a year ago  in the press freedom index hong kong was  was 80th and last week it saw the  biggest fall of any territory in the  world  to 148 so this is not only what johnny  is inheriting but also what he shares  responsibility for as a top official  throughout her tenure well let me throw  that then to victor  given the level of unpopularity of  carrie lam the legacy that she's left  and how she moved closer to beijing and  the perception of john lee is being very  close to beijing what challenges is he  likely to face trying to get hong  kongers on side  i think mr lee has huge challenges he  need to bring the society together he  need to reopen hong kong to  foreign business interests he hopefully  will need to visit the united states and  many other european countries and japan  and australia canada you name it to make  sure that hong kong is here to stay at  the one country to system will remain in  effect up to 2047 and hong kong will  continue to practice  capitalism with hong kong  characteristics with rule of law with  the hong kong dollar you've talked about  his engagement with various different  capitals i mean he can't set foot in the  u.s because he's sanctioned by the u.s  and obviously the crackdown in hong kong  has left a bit of taste in the mouth of  of many foreign leaders  i'm curious about where steve you think  this might be going in terms of how hong  kong is going to be perceived in the  eyes of the rest of the world and and  and how china is playing its cards here  well i think china just created  a moral hazard for  uh leaders of democratic democratic  countries in europe and in north america  if they accept john needs to visit  then they basically are telling  authoritarian countries in the world  that if they then make somebody who has  done something particularly odious  pretty put him in a position of  authority and whatever sanctions imposed  on 